Apple this week released the full and final version of iOS 27.1, but it is only available for the foldable iPhone Duo that launched today.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iOS-27-1.jpg" alt="" width="1673" height="940" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072343" /><br/>
iPhone Duo already ships with iOS 27.1 preinstalled, but users need to update to a newer build of iOS 27.1 while setting up the device, and that newer build is available for download as an <a href="https://updates.cdn-apple.com/2026FallFCS/3f41a447-d8a6-48ae-83ae-056a64f57fd4/iPhone19,4_27.1_24A94425_Restore.ipsw">IPSW file</a> as of Thursday. Apple's release notes for the newer build simply mention unspecified "bug fixes for your iPhone." iOS 27.1 has <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/100100">no security fixes</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Apple jumped ahead to an iOS 27.2 beta for all other compatible iPhone models, and the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/19/apple-releases-ios-27-2-rc/">iOS 27.2 Release Candidate</a> is compatible with the iPhone Duo too, so all of the models will be able to upgrade to iOS 27.2 for consistency.<br/>
<br/>
iOS 27.1 was likely kept exclusive to the iPhone Duo so that Apple could prevent certain Duo-specific software features from leaking before the device launched.<br/>
<br/>
